  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe'll employ a two-stage strategy combining convex optimization for initial solution and local refinement to improve the best-known upper bound for C\u2085. First, a convex relaxation of the problem is solved to obtain a feasible continuous solution, then converted into a step function and further optimized through local adjustments. This approach leverages the strength of convex solvers for an initial guess while addressing the non-convex nature of the step function constraint through iterative refinement.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nDefine variables h \u2208 [0,1]^n with \u2211h = n/2. Convex relaxation replaces h binary with continuous [0,1]. The objective becomes minimizing C = max_k (h \u22c6 (1 - h))[k] * (2/n), where \u22c6 is linear convolution.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialize with n_points=70 and read initial_h_values if available.\nb. Solve convex relaxation using CVXPY:\n   - Variables h \u2208 [0,1]^n\n   - Constraints: \u2211h = n/2\n   - Objective: minimize C s.t. all convolution values \u2264 C\nc. Threshold h_computed to binary step function.\nd. Adjust step positions to satisfy \u2211h = n/2 exactly.\ne. Perform local refinement by perturbing step positions within feasibility constraints.\nf. Evaluate and keep track of the best solution.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Initialize with a blocky step function derived from the convex solution.\n- Use SQP for local optimization focusing on step locations.\n- Perform gradient checks on the objective function.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Convex solve: ~50s\n- Local search: ~10s per iteration\n- 100 iterations feasible within 600s\n- Track best solution and return it on budget exhaustion.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Ensure h \u2208 [0,1]^n and \u2211h = n/2 exactly.\n- Recompute overlap integral to validate.",
